1374:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     /* This code basically does three things:
1375:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c **** 
1376:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     +) If the incoming segment contains data that is the next
1377:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     in-sequence data, this data is passed to the application. This
1378:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     might involve trimming the first edge of the data. The rcv_nxt
1379:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     variable and the advertised window are adjusted.
1380:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c **** 
1381:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     +) If the incoming segment has data that is above the next
1382:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     sequence number expected (->rcv_nxt), the segment is placed on
1383:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     the ->ooseq queue. This is done by finding the appropriate
1384:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     place in the ->ooseq queue (which is ordered by sequence
1385:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     number) and trim the segment in both ends if needed. An
1386:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     immediate ACK is sent to indicate that we received an
1387:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     out-of-sequence segment.
1388:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c **** 
1389:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     +) Finally, we check if the first segment on the ->ooseq queue
1390:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     now is in sequence (i.e., if rcv_nxt >= ooseq->seqno). If
1391:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     rcv_nxt > ooseq->seqno, we must trim the first edge of the
1392:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     segment on ->ooseq before we adjust rcv_nxt. The data in the
1393:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     segments that are now on sequence are chained onto the
1394:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     incoming segment so that we only need to call the application
1395:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     once.
1396:./lwip-2.1.2/src/core/tcp_in.c ****     */
